efuting_outcome":"The gap is 3 characters or less AND both labelings clear their null. The ripple at that window is credited to token position, not to counting characters."}],"sampling":"No text is generated. Every measurement reads hidden activations from one deterministic forward pass per document per wrap width, so there are no temperature or top-p settings. Layers swept: 0 through 24, where 0 is the embedding output and 24 is the last transformer layer (both MEMORY, Pythia paper, arXiv 2304.01373, table 1); a mismatch against the downloaded model's own config halts the run first. Ridge strength is chosen by five-fold cross-validation on the 36 training documents (the five-fold choice is a GUESS; the question only requires a grid past 1000), from the grid {1, 10, 100, 1000, 3000, 10000, 30000, 100000} (GUESS, exact points not fixed by the question). Two separate layer selections run: one pooled over all nine wrap widths (the main layer, used for every ripple test and the range check), one using only the width-140 documents (the anchor layer, used only for the anchor gate). Label-shuffle null for the three main ripple tests: 200 draws per window per labeling (character-count and token-position; the 200 figure is the question's own GUESS), each draw shuffling one document's labels within that document only, fixed base random seed 0 (GUESS, arbitrary, for reproducibility only). Label-shuffle null for the represented-range check: 50 draws per bin, 8 bins, window 280 only, 400 draws total, same seeding convention (50 draws and 8 bins are fixed by the question, not guessed). Anchor dispersion: 5 resplits of the 36 training documents (fixed by the question), each holding out 6 documents (GUESS split size) as a fresh internal test set, re-running the width-140 layer selection and fit on each split; the spread across these 5 values sets the anchor band's half-width. Each window's ripple profile length (how many periods the discrete Fourier transform searches) is committed by a calibration rule, not guessed: on training documents only, take the largest label value that occurs at that window's wrap width (or width plus one, for line-final newline tokens), and commit that count before any test-document ripple number is read. n per cell: 12 test documents per window for every main ripple test and its null; 36 training documents pooled over nine widths for the main layer selection; 36 training documents at width 140 only for the anchor layer selection and its 5 resplits; 8 bins of 35 label values each, from the 12 test documents at window 280, for the represented-range check.","layers_strengths":"Main layer: best held-out fit, swept over layers 0-24, ridge strength chosen from a grid extending past 1000 (exact points GUESS), fit pooled over all nine wrap widths on the 36 training documents, scored on the 12 test documents. Anchor layer: the same sweep and the same grid, but fit and scored using only the width-140 documents; used only for the anchor gate, never for any ripple test. Both selections pick the layer and strength with the best held-out fit on their own training data; whether the chosen strength sits at an interior grid point or at an edge is recorded for each selection.","budget_usd":4,"does_not_test":["Does not test any checkpoint besides step143000, or whether a ripple is a stable feature versus training-time debris; the checkpoint sweep is out of scope by the question's own statement.","Does not test any model besides this one 410-million-parameter substrate cell; other models or sizes are out of scope by the question's own statement.","Does not test any causal intervention on the model; only passive reading of activations is in scope by the question's own statement.","Does not re-run the prior fitted-probe cross-check from seed D1; the question drops it because its agreement was empty, its fit never converged, and no null was ever computable for it.","Does not decide INVARIANT versus PROPORTIONAL when exactly one window clears its null; that case is recorded UNDETERMINED by design, not resolved by collecting more data.","The exact ridge-strength grid points past 1000 are a GUESS; a coarser grid could miss the true best strength and understate every held-out fit quality, main layer and anchor layer alike.","Five-fold cross-validation for choosing ridge strength is a GUESS; the question fixes only a grid past 1000, not a fold count, and a different fold count could shift the winning strength slightly.","The base random seed (0) for every shuffle draw is a GUESS; a different seed could move a borderline window across its own clearing threshold, which is why the result is read from the invariance fork across windows, not from one window's raw pass or fail.","The 6-document holdout size for each of the 5 anchor resplits is a GUESS; a larger holdout would widen the measured dispersion and could turn a narrow miss into a pass, or the reverse.","The 10-character invariance cutoff and the 0.1 ratio cutoff are guesses carried unchanged from the question's own text; a true wavelength drifting by, say, 12 characters would be called non-invariant even if it were the same pattern plus noise.","The 3-character token-versus-character margin is a guess carried unchanged from the question's own text; a real character effect could be misattributed to token position near that margin, or the reverse.","The 200-draw shuffle null, per window per labeling, is a guess carried unchanged from the question's own text; it sets the finest detectable false-positive rate near 0.5 percent per window, so a real but rarer ripple could be missed.","The rule that derives each window's ripple profile length from training documents' own label range is this design's own addition, since the question requires a committed calibration step but does not spell out the derivation; a different derivation could search a different top period and change which periods the statistic can find.","The $4 budget is a GUESS built from this project's own prior costs for similar three-window runs on this checkpoint (about $0.55 to $0.6 actual), plus one hour of rented A100 time at $1.99 and headroom for one retry; a slower checkpoint download or a stuck resplit loop could still push real cost higher.","The hardware assumption (one A100 40 GB SXM4 box, $1.99 per hour, us-west-2, whole run finishing inside one hour) is a GUESS about capacity and speed; if the box lacks capacity or the run overruns the hour, the launcher's own cap governs, not this estimate."]}}
